          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fractions in Disguise"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.1,
            0.831,
            0.156
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Profit percentage is calculated based on the revenue (sales amount) when we want to know “How much (net) profit did I make on my overall revenue?”."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.156,
            0.832,
            0.259
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose, in a month she made sales of ₹1,50,000. The cost of buying the goods was ₹1,00,000. So, the gross profit is ₹50,000. The monthly expenses amounted to ₹5,000. The net profit is ₹45,000. Out of monthly revenue (₹1,50,000), the net profit percentage is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{45000}{150000} \\times 100 = \\frac{3}{10} \\times 100 = 30\\%."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.296,
            0.23,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Taxes"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.326,
            0.659,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Tax rates, like the GST (Goods and Services Tax) rate or the Income Tax rates, are also specified as percentages. You may have noticed GST mentioned as part of bills. This means that the tax is part of the amount we pay and this amount goes to the government."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.448,
            0.658,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Check if the calculations are correct in the bill shown."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.673,
            0.278,
            0.89,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": [
